# CI Infrastructure
#
# State holds resource metadata in plaintext; a tfvars would hold the billing
# account id. Neither belongs in git.
# Patterns are infra/**/ rather than infra/ so a second stack -- a different
# gcloud project alongside infra/ci -- is covered the day it appears, instead of
# leaking state or a tfvars until someone notices. `**/` matches zero or more
# directories, so these still cover infra/foo.tfvars as well as infra/ci/foo.tfvars.
infra/**/terraform.tfstate
infra/**/terraform.tfstate.backup
infra/**/*.tfvars
infra/**/*.tfvars.json
# The provider plugin cache: megabytes of vendored binaries, re-fetchable.
infra/**/.terraform/**
#
# NOT ignored, deliberately: infra/**/.terraform.lock.hcl
#
# It was ignored, which defeats the one thing it exists for. The lock records
# the exact provider versions a config was validated against (google 7.45.0
# here), so `terraform init` resolves the same ones on every machine. Ignored,
# each init is free to pick a newer provider, and a plan that was clean for you
# can differ for CI or for the next person -- the failure mode being a
# surprising diff on infrastructure rather than a build error.
.grok/

# e2e run artefacts: pytest empties this on every run (see docker-compose e2e)
.e2e/

# exo memory
.exo/